module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.Lie.Semisimple.Basic
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Lie.Weights.Basic
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Basis.Defs
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Dual.Defs

/-!
# Rational highest-weight modules and their integral weights

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Section 23h, printed pp.508 and 511;
the rational highest-weight input expanded in the DAG's Verma-quotient proof
(blueprint: lem-sfb-rational-highest-weight-modules).

The roots, normalized coroots, simple base and integral lattice are specified
by their defining conditions. Weight spaces are ordinary simultaneous eigenspaces.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

namespace LieAlgebra

/-- The integral weight lattice has the fundamental-weight basis, and every dominant
integral weight has a finite-dimensional simple highest-weight module over `ℚ`.

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Section 23h, printed pp.508 and 511,
with the split rational highest-weight argument recorded in the DAG
(blueprint: lem-sfb-rational-highest-weight-modules).

Proof plan:
1. Use `lem-sfb-split-semisimple-root-decomposition` to obtain the reduced
crystallographic root pairing for evaluation. By
`lem-sfb-root-base-rational-integral-spans`, the simple coroots are a rational
basis and every coroot is an integral combination of them. Its dual basis is
therefore a `ℤ`-basis of the integral weight lattice; roots belong to this lattice.
2. For dominant `λ`, form the rational Verma quotient of
`lem-sfb-verma-monomial-weight-basis`. Its highest vector is nonzero, its highest
weight space is one-dimensional, and all weights lie below `λ`.
3. Put `nᵢ = λ(hᵢ)`. The commutator identity
`[eᵢ,fᵢ^m]v = m(nᵢ-m+1)fᵢ^(m-1)v` and `[eⱼ,fᵢ]=0` for distinct indices
show that each `fᵢ^(nᵢ+1)v` is killed by the positive subalgebra.
4. Quotient by the submodules generated by these singular vectors. Their weights
are strictly below `λ`, so the highest vector survives. Ordinary weight spaces
and their finite dimensions descend to this nonzero cyclic quotient.
5. The simple root operators act locally nilpotently: their adjoint actions are
nilpotent on the Lie algebra, locally nilpotent on its enveloping algebra, and
`x^m u v = ∑ k, (m choose k) (ad x)^k(u) x^(m-k)v` propagates the generator's
nilpotence to every vector.
6. Apply `lem-sfb-integrable-upper-bounded-weight-finiteness` to get finite dimension.
7. Quotient by a proper invariant subspace of largest dimension. The quotient is
simple, and its cyclic highest vector remains nonzero. A rational Cartan element
separating the finitely many weights gives spectral projections by interpolation;
these preserve the subspace, so ordinary weight decompositions descend.
8. Keep precisely the nonzero quotient weight spaces. They include `λ`, lie below
it, and are integral since roots are integral. All constructions took place over
`ℚ`. For the zero Lie algebra use `ℚ`, its zero action and the vector `1`;
the empty product provides the generator and the only weight is zero.

The independent, spanning family in the conclusion is the internal direct sum
of ordinary weight spaces. The final normalization identifies the rank-zero
module with `ℚ`, carrying the highest vector to `1`. -/
theorem exists_rational_highestWeight_module
    (g : Type u) [LieRing g] [LieAlgebra ℚ g] [FiniteDimensional ℚ g]
    [HasTrivialRadical ℚ g] (H : LieSubalgebra ℚ g) [IsLieAbelian H]
    (hmax : ∀ A : LieSubalgebra ℚ g, IsLieAbelian A → H ≤ A → A = H)
    (hsplit : (⨆ χ : Module.Dual ℚ H,
      (LieModule.weightSpace g (χ : H → ℚ)).toSubmodule) = ⊤)
    (R : Set (Module.Dual ℚ H))
    (hR : ∀ α : Module.Dual ℚ H, α ∈ R ↔
      α ≠ 0 ∧ LieModule.weightSpace g (α : H → ℚ) ≠ ⊥)
    (c : R → H)
    (hc : ∀ α : R,
      (c α : g) ∈ Submodule.span ℚ {z : g | ∃ x y : g,
        x ∈ LieModule.weightSpace g (α.1 : H → ℚ) ∧
        y ∈ LieModule.weightSpace g ((-α.1) : H → ℚ) ∧ ⁅x, y⁆ = z} ∧
      α.1 (c α) = 2)
    (Δ : Finset R)
    (hbase : LinearIndependent ℚ (fun i : Δ ↦ i.1.1))
    (hcoords : ∀ α : R,
      (∃ n : Δ → ℕ, α.1 = ∑ i : Δ, (n i : ℚ) • i.1.1) ∨
      (∃ n : Δ → ℕ, -α.1 = ∑ i : Δ, (n i : ℚ) • i.1.1))
    (P : Submodule ℤ (Module.Dual ℚ H))
    (hP : ∀ μ : Module.Dual ℚ H, μ ∈ P ↔
      ∀ α : R, ∃ n : ℤ, μ (c α) = (n : ℚ)) :
    ∃ ω : Module.Basis Δ ℤ P,
      (∀ i j : Δ,
        (i = j → (ω i : Module.Dual ℚ H) (c j) = 1) ∧
        (i ≠ j → (ω i : Module.Dual ℚ H) (c j) = 0)) ∧
      ∀ lam : P, (∀ i : Δ, 0 ≤ (lam : Module.Dual ℚ H) (c i)) →
        ∃ (M : Type u) (_ : AddCommGroup M) (_ : Module ℚ M)
          (_ : LieRingModule g M) (_ : LieModule ℚ g M),
          FiniteDimensional ℚ M ∧ LieModule.IsIrreducible ℚ g M ∧
          ∃ v : M, v ≠ 0 ∧
            (∀ h : H, ⁅(h : g), v⁆ = (lam : Module.Dual ℚ H) h • v) ∧
            (∀ α : R, (∃ n : Δ → ℕ, α.1 = ∑ i : Δ, (n i : ℚ) • i.1.1) →
              ∀ x : g, x ∈ LieModule.weightSpace g (α.1 : H → ℚ) → ⁅x, v⁆ = 0) ∧
            LieSubmodule.lieSpan ℚ g ({v} : Set M) = ⊤ ∧
            (∃ Ω : Finset (Module.Dual ℚ H),
              (lam : Module.Dual ℚ H) ∈ Ω ∧
              (∀ μ ∈ Ω, μ ∈ P ∧ ∃ n : Δ → ℕ,
                μ = (lam : Module.Dual ℚ H) - ∑ i : Δ, (n i : ℚ) • i.1.1) ∧
              (∀ μ ∈ Ω, LieModule.weightSpace M (μ : H → ℚ) ≠ ⊥) ∧
              iSupIndep (fun μ : Ω ↦
                (LieModule.weightSpace M (μ.1 : H → ℚ)).toSubmodule) ∧
              (⨆ μ : Ω, (LieModule.weightSpace M (μ.1 : H → ℚ)).toSubmodule) = ⊤) ∧
            (Subsingleton g → ∃ e : M ≃ₗ[ℚ] ℚ,
              e v = 1 ∧ ∀ (x : g) (m : M), ⁅x, m⁆ = 0) := by
  sorry

end LieAlgebra
